t services into  separate services  let's take a look at the example over  here we've pulled out a couple of  component services the newsletter and  the shop  as we continue this migration and pull  out a third service you start to see the  connections between the services develop  as we pull out a fourth service the  connections between them  deepen further  we've now completed the migration to  microservices  while we've started to address some of  the issues with scalability  extensibility and coupling we haven't  done so completely  we've improved on scalability but  there's still a question of whether the  communication between services is  synchronous  or asynchronous that makes a difference  we've improved on extensibility  however the new services that we add  may still require that we make updates  to  existing services that are going to  communicate with that new service  and lastly while we've made coupling  looser  it's possible that different services  may still have couple dependencies  on a message queue for instance which  you may want to take a dependency on  if you're looking to protect against  intermittent failure  of services  in addition to the three problems that  we've only solved partially  microservices also introduce three new  problems  for one there's a spiderweb of  point-to-point integrations like you saw  in the previous slide  there's the potential for expensive  inter-service communication  especially if the communication between  services is full style say via polling  there's also the potential for  non-uniform communication format  between services  we continue our evolution onto event  driven architectures  but first let's start by describing what  is an event  there's three concepts to think about  here occurrences  events and actions in the operation of a  software system  an occurrence is when something happens  a statement of fact about something  happening  an event is a record expressing that  occurrence and its context  and an action will take place based on  an occurrence  which is communicated to a service  through an event  now events originate asynchronously and  with events a producer has no  expectation  of how the event might be handled by a  consumer  let's take a look at a typical  event-driven architecture or an eda  multiple event producers go ahead and  produce  events and send them on to event  intermediaries  at the other end event consumers  subscribe to the events  in particular that they're interested in  from specific producers  now eda's solved several of the problems  that we identified earlier  [Music]  let's start by looking at scalability  take a look at these two systems that  you see in front of you  one can handle 200 transactions a second  and the second can handle  100 transactions a second now with just  a micro services architecture  where the communication between them is  synchronous  a request response style the weakest  link dictates the peak  throughput of the system  but if you then graduate onto event  driven microservices  the weakest link now dictates the  sustained throughput of the system  let's take a look at extensibility and  how it's easier to add services with  event driven architectures  we have a couple of event producers and  a couple of event consumers  we're going to go ahead and add another  event consumer  when we add an event consumer no changes  need to be made  to any producer thus making  extensibility a lot easier  let me highlight some of the other  benefits of event driven architectures  we can now decouple from dependencies  completely the event intermediary can  handle intermittent unavailability of  services  there's no more spider web no more  point-to-point integrations  event producers can just fire events and  forget about them  events are pushed and this replaces  pull-based  polling and lastly there's an  opportunity to introduce a standardized  format  a uniform communication format between  services  for instance cloud events format with an  ideal event-driven architecture system  users no longer have to be experts in  distributed computing  they can take event ingestion delivery  security  authorization observability and error  handling for granted  and just focus on their application code  for their use case  this brings us to google cloud's new  offerings in this space  you're already familiar with some of  google cloud's offerings to help you  with microservices  cloudrun cloudrun helps you run any  stateless container on google's fully  managed infrastructure  and there's cloudrun for anthos which  helps you run any stateless container on  google kubernetes engine  is k native conformant and helps you  achieve portability  to other clouds or on-prem today we  introduce to you  two new capabilities to help you with  event driven micro services  events for cloud run and events for  cloud cloudra inferentials  both these capabilities help you easily  build event driven architectures  let's take a look at both of these  capabilities one after the other  with events for cloud run you can set up  triggers  to act on events sourced from all google  cloud sources  via cloud audit logs for instance  cloud storage a popular google cloud  source via cloud audit logs  you can also act on events from custom  applications  that publish events to cloud pops up  events for cloud run delivers these  events to cloud run targets  let's now talk about events for cloudron  for anthos  events for quadrant for anthos is  k-native-based and incorporates the  concepts of k-native brokers  and triggers with events for quadrant  for anthos  you can act on events sourced from  all google cloud sources via cloud art  logs  cloud storage and cloud scheduler in  particular which are native integrations  custom applications that are publishing  events to cloud pub sub  and also custom events you can target  cloudrun for anthos targets kubernetes  services  and also any url with both events for  cloud run and events for cloud run for  enthos  they both handle event ingestion  delivery  security authorization observability and  error handling for you  they both have the same delivery  guarantees as cloud pub sub  at least once delivery for example  they both provide on-demand scalability  and cost  and you can operate both of them through  a ui cli  or api interface  both events for cloud run and events for  cloud9 for anthos deliver events in  cloud events format  this also allows you the user to use  cloud events available sdks in multiple  languages  to be able to work with your cloudants  we also provide libraries to help you  unmarshal the event data  within climates i'd now like to take you  through a sentiment analysis demo  showing you  the use of events for cloud run and  events for cloud9 for enthus  we're going to take a complex image and  process it  with an event driven architecture system  that we're going to build  for example take a look at this image  here of a family  now the holidays bring out all kinds of  different emotions in different members  of the family  what we're going to try to do here is  from an input image  draw out each family member  and determine what their sentiments are  what their sentiment score is  all using event driven architectures now  to accomplish such an event-driven  architecture  there's a few micro services that we're  going to need  a cloud storage bucket  a firestore database  and a few different compute services  these can be cloud run services or cloud  run for antho services  to start we need one service which can  help us extract the bounding boxes  of faces from the original image  we need another that can extract face  images  from those bounding boxes  a third that can run those faces through  an open  neural network exchange model and then  generate an emotion score from them  a fourth that can write those scores to  a firestore database  and a fifth to render a user interface  now let's take a look at 